s at play  and simon what did you make of the  response because some people have been  calling for a windfall tax on oil and  gas companies that could then be passed  on in discounts to consumers they were  pointing out shells big profits today  their profits went up to nearly 20  billion dollars for example would you  have welcomed that move or would you  have worried that that would take away  their potential investment into  renewables into greener energy sources  it's always very complicated to try and  work out exactly where the fossil fuel  companies  will put their money i think the  question of windfall tax it is an option  the government can consider but really  it's just a short-term solution again  the systemic problem as as jen was  saying is the fact that we use too much  gas and gas is an international  commodity trade there are markets which  are very volatile and open to  manipulation we saw just yesterday we  were expecting gas flows from russia  into europe and then mysteriously they  stopped so we're still at the mercy of  this manipulation  and so really talking about windfall  taxes or other issues around the fossil  fuel industry is really just very  short-term and the by far the best  solution is to use less gas and move  away from it entirely in the long term  would you agree with that jen and what  do you make of this idea of a windfall  tax i mean the chancellor made it clear  he wanted to shield north sea oil and  gas producers for the sake of both  energy security and job security and to  to allow those companies to continue to  invest in the green transition what do  you make of that stance  i don't think that companies should be  earning record profits off the back of  the poor it's uh  really difficult for them probably to  try to justify record profits while  people are looking at the incredible  increase in gas prices that they'll be  facing  so a windfall tax probably sends a good  message that if they are going to renew  invest in 