aginative  solutions and only we did hear about a  shared ownership of a wind farm  and in bristol local communities are  investing into solar projects in order  to move off grid so simon do you think  an idea like this a kind of shared  ownership of a wind turbine could could  take off and give consumers an  alternative way of getting their bills  down  it certainly is a very beneficial  approach and there are countries  such as denmark and germany where  community energy has been very very  successful  and not only is it a way of people  investing um and getting a return it's  as a way of them getting cheaper energy  from renewables it also really helps to  build a sense of interest and engagement  with a whole energy question and people  tend to be more energy efficient more  interested in insulation to bring their  bills down and just generally more  welcoming of the transition so i think  community energy really does have a very  valuable role potentially  what do you make of this i mean the one  issue is it does require people to put a  lot of money in up front before they get  the benefit and people are suffering  this squeeze on their finances at the  moment so how practical do you think it  could be  it could be that such a community model  like this might be a way to get into the  energy market that wouldn't be possible  otherwise if you think about putting  solar panels on your house that's a big  upfront cost that  we can't afford right now not a lot of  people can  even if you're going to earn that money  back over time so  something like this where you pay a  little bit and then you start to see the  benefit that might be possible for a lot  of people and it might sort of demystify  energy you know like where we wonder  where our food comes from this might  help people kind of understand where  their energy comes from  okay well we must leave it there for  today but jen allen and simon crown  agree and really interesting to get your  views thanks very 